const tipEnglish = <String, String>{
  'trackDatesHelps': 'Noting your period dates in the app helps you see your own pattern and gives a clinician clear facts.',
  'periodLongerThanSeven': 'A period that lasts longer than 7 days is worth mentioning to a clinician.',
  'bleedingBetweenPeriods': 'Bleeding between periods is worth mentioning to a clinician, so the cause can be looked into.',
  'irregularCheckIn': 'Cycles shorter than 21 or longer than 35 days are called irregular. Mention this to a clinician, who can look at your dates with you.',
  'stoppedThreeMonths': 'A period that stops for more than three months without a clear reason is worth a clinician\'s check, whatever your age.',
  'irregularWithOtherSigns': 'Irregular periods together with other changes, such as acne or extra hair growth, are worth mentioning to a clinician.',
  'periodPainSeeClinician': 'Period pain that stops you doing your usual daily activities is a reason to see a clinician.',
  'severePainUrgent': 'If pelvic or period pain is severe or worse than usual and painkillers have not helped, ask for urgent medical advice.',
  'symptomDiaryBeforeVisit': 'Before a visit about period problems, a diary of your symptoms across the whole cycle gives your clinician a detailed picture of what happens and when.',
  'heavyOrTired': 'Periods that keep you from daily activities, or leave you tired or short of breath a lot, can be signs of heavy periods. A clinician can help.',
  'pmsDiary': 'Keeping a diary of your symptoms for at least two cycles helps a clinician see whether they follow your period.',
  'headachesAroundPeriod': 'Headaches that come regularly before or during your period are worth mentioning to a clinician. A headache diary next to your period dates helps.',
  'urineInfectionSeeClinician': 'If you think you may have a urine infection, it is important to see a clinician. A urine test can tell.',
  'leakingUrineTellClinician': 'If you leak urine, it is worth telling a clinician, whatever the type or cause.',
  'cervicalScreeningTip': 'Cervical screening looks for early cell changes in the cervix. Ask your clinic whether you are due.',
  'ttcOneYear': 'If a year of trying has passed without a pregnancy, it is a good idea to see a clinician and have the common causes checked.',
  'ttcAgeSixMonths': 'At 35 or older, it is advised to see a clinician after six months of trying, not twelve.',
  'ttcIrregularNoWait': 'With irregular or no periods, it is worth talking to a clinician about trying to conceive without waiting a full year.',
  'regularSexTakesTime': 'Most couples where the woman is under 40 conceive within a year of regular sex every 2 to 3 days. It can take time.',
  'testingTooEarly': 'Testing very early can give a false negative, because the pregnancy hormone may not have built up yet. Follow the test\'s own instructions.',
  'folicAcidBeforePregnancy': 'Folic acid, a B vitamin, is advised from at least a month before pregnancy. Ask a clinician how to get enough.',
  'talkBeforeTrying': 'It is a good idea to talk to a clinician before trying to get pregnant. They can help you get your body ready.',
  'tryingIsStressful': 'Trying to conceive can be stressful. Counselling and support groups may help you cope; you can ask a clinician about them.',
  'movementsChangeCallNow': 'If your baby moves less than usual or the usual pattern changes, call your midwife or maternity unit at once, even in the middle of the night.',
  'firstMovementsBy24': 'You should start to feel your baby move between 16 and 24 weeks. If there has been no movement by 24 weeks, tell your midwife.',
  'preeclampsiaSigns': 'A severe headache that painkillers do not ease, vision problems, pain below the ribs or sudden swelling need checking at once. Call your maternity unit.',
  'antenatalChecksMatter': 'Blood pressure and urine checks at antenatal visits can find changes you cannot feel, so try to keep every appointment.',
  'screeningVersusDiagnostic': 'Two kinds of tests are offered to every pregnant woman: screening tests, which show a chance, and follow-up tests, which give a firmer answer. Ask which suit you.',
  'glucoseScreening': 'All pregnant women should be screened for gestational diabetes. Ask your midwife or doctor when yours is planned.',
  'rhBloodGroup': 'A simple blood test shows your blood type and Rh status, usually at your first antenatal visit. Ask what yours is and whether it matters for your care.',
  'mainScanBetween18And21': 'A detailed scan is usually done between 18 and 21 weeks. It checks your baby\'s growth and looks for certain conditions.',
  'severeSicknessTellEarly': 'If you are being sick often and cannot keep food down, tell your midwife or doctor as soon as you can, so they can help you keep fluids down.',
  'itchTellMidwife': 'Itching is common in pregnancy and is usually not serious. Still, tell your midwife or doctor about it so it can be checked.',
  'pretermLabourSigns': 'Signs of early labour include contractions every 10 minutes or more often, leaking fluid or bleeding, pelvic pressure and a low, dull backache. Contact your provider.',
  'tirednessInPregnancy': 'Tiredness in pregnancy has many causes, and anaemia is one of them. Mention ongoing tiredness to your midwife or doctor.',
  'lowMoodPerinatal': 'Feeling low is common in pregnancy and after birth, and support is available. Tell your midwife, doctor or health visitor, even if you only have some of the signs.',
  'anxietyInPregnancy': 'Anxiety in pregnancy can often be helped with talking therapy. Tell your midwife or doctor how you really feel.',
  'warningSignsPregnancyAndYear': 'Some warning signs need care at once, in pregnancy and for a year after birth: a headache that will not go away, vision changes, fever, trouble breathing, severe belly pain.',
  'postnatalCheckSixToEight': 'A postnatal check around 6 to 8 weeks after birth makes sure you feel well and are recovering. Write down your questions to take along.',
  'painfulFeedsGetHelp': 'If you are putting off feeds because breastfeeding hurts, get help from a lactation consultant.',
  'tirednessAfterBirthThyroid': 'Tiredness after birth has many causes. Thyroid inflammation affects about 1 in 20 women in the first year after birth, so mention lasting tiredness to a clinician.',
  'bleedingUpToSixWeeks': 'Spotting or bleeding like a period, on and off, can last up to six weeks after birth. Tell a clinician if you are worried.',
  'babyBluesPassing': 'Feeling down, anxious or irritable for a few days after birth is very common and usually passes within 2 weeks. If it lasts longer, tell a clinician.',
  'periodChangesFirstSign': 'A change in your periods is usually one of the first signs of perimenopause. They may come more or less often, and be heavier or lighter.',
  'twelveMonthsMeansMenopause': 'Menopause means 12 months without a period. In the years before, cycles often vary, with longer or shorter gaps.',
  'bleedingAfterMenopause': 'Bleeding after menopause, meaning 12 months with no period, is important to tell a clinician about, so the cause can be found.',
  'hotFlushesTalkOptions': 'If lifestyle changes are not enough for hot flushes, contact a clinician. They will talk through your symptoms, history and preferences.',
  'boneStrengthMenopause': 'Loss of bone density is more likely around menopause. Ask a clinician about bone health and what suits you.',
  'drynessManyTreatments': 'There are many treatments for vaginal dryness. It is worth asking a clinician what could suit you.',
  'heartbeatAroundMenopause': 'A faster or more noticeable heartbeat can be part of perimenopause and menopause. Note when it happens and tell a clinician.',
  'lowMoodMenopause': 'Low mood and anxiety can be part of the menopause transition. If they are hard to manage, talk to a clinician.',
};

